WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.850
Is it perfect timing, cool vibes, or can Spider-Man
just not miss at the box office?

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:09.561
For all these reasons and more, "Across the
Spider-Verse" blew the competition away.

00:00:09.561 --> 00:00:13.070
Some superheroes are pretty obscure – and
some are Spider-Man.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.279
Regardless, the first "Spider-Verse" asked
a lot from viewers, centering on a Spidey

00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:19.270
who'd never been tested on the big screen.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:23.430
In addition to focusing on Miles Morales,
it was also one of the first modern superhero

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:27.940
movies to embrace the concept of the multiverse,
bringing a whole crew of obscure takes on

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.769
Spider-Man and making them front and center.

00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:34.360
Compared to live-action tentpoles starring
the likes of Tom Holland or Andrew Garfield

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.570
as the familiar Peter Parker, this Spider-Man
adventure arrived somewhat under-the-radar.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.290
That's not the case this time around.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:45.700
Thanks to great reviews that called it maybe
the best Spider-Man film ever made, the original

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:50.320
movie won over legions of new fans in their
living rooms, building a groundswell of excitement

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.510
that had the sequel opening to a far larger
potential audience.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:57.590
As a result, "Across the Spider-Verse" will
likely surpass the box office total of its

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.780
predecessor in a fraction of the time.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.190
"I've leveled up my whole thing, see?"

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:07.799
With a 2019 Oscar win for best animated feature
and a plethora of other accolades, "Into the

00:01:07.799 --> 00:01:11.560
Spider-Verse" was too successful to be called
a cult movie, but it nonetheless collected

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.170
a substantial cult of die-hards.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:17.700
As Slashfilm puts it, the sequel's success
"speaks volumes about how the audience for

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.180
the first film exploded over the last 4 years."

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:25.049
Keep in mind, this trend is likely to continue
— "Across the Spider-Verse" will undoubtedly

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:29.021
play well in fans' homes for months to come,
winning over yet more new converts who will

00:01:29.021 --> 00:01:32.049
be pounding at the theater doors in March
2024.

00:01:32.049 --> 00:01:35.970
Thanks to this movie's many cliffhangers,
no one who sees it will want to miss the grand

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.499
conclusion promised by its own upcoming sequel,
"Beyond the Spider-Verse."

00:01:40.499 --> 00:01:44.420
Out of all the recent Spider–Man movies,
"Into the Spider-Verse" was the only one to

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.040
have made less than $700 million worldwide.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:53.420
Nonetheless, its reported final haul of over
$375 million represented a healthy profit

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.240
when compared to the movie's $90 million budget.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.039
For over two decades now, Spider-Man movies
have been as close to a sure bet as you can

00:02:00.039 --> 00:02:04.060
get in Hollywood, regardless of who stars
in them or how well they're received by critics

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.190
and audiences.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:09.460
Peter Parker's appearances in the Marvel Cinematic
Universe have also come in some of its most

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:13.700
lucrative installments — "Captain America:
Civil War," "Avengers: Infinity War," and

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.430
"Avengers: Endgame" — which, combined, made
about $6 billion.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:22.480
The original Spider-Verse opened relatively
modestly for Sony, netting over $35 million

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.920
at the global box office and winning the weekend
on the back of its familiar branding.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:31.030
After that movie won so many fans over, the
sequel's opening weekend blew it out of the

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:36.446
water, raking in well over three times as
much money with $120.5 million.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.750
When "Into the Spider-Verse" premiered, moviegoers
who weren't steeped in the source material

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.629
may not have been exposed to the concept of
a multiverse.

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:47.769
While there were stray sci-fi movies and tv
shows that dealt with alternate realities,

00:02:47.769 --> 00:02:52.330
the word "multiverse" was rarely if ever used,
and the concept was far from the center of

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:53.330
the zeitgeist.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:54.330
"Are you from another dimension?

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:58.010
Like a parallel universe where things are
like this universe but different and you're

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.540
spider-man in that universe but somehow traveled
to this universe but you don't know how?"

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:06.739
Although the notion was vaguely referenced
in 2016's "Doctor Strange," the MCU's multiverse

00:03:06.739 --> 00:03:10.659
didn't really get kicking until after 2019's
"Avengers: Endgame."

00:03:10.659 --> 00:03:14.970
Phases 4 and 5's inter-dimensional stories
like "Loki" and "Doctor Strange in the Multiverse

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:18.500
of Madness" brought the concept heavily into
the overarching plot.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:23.376
That means "Into the Spider-Verse" should
get substantial credit for making multiverses mainstream.

00:03:23.376 --> 00:03:28.290
Consider this: Only four years after that
film served as a multiverse primer, "Everything

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.959
Everywhere All at Once" — a live-action
film about the concept — won seven Academy

00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:34.189
Awards, including best picture.

00:03:34.189 --> 00:03:37.980
In the years between the first and second
"Spider-Verse" films, the notion of infinite

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:42.760
planes of existence has become commonplace
enough that it almost feels like a cliche.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:46.500
Audiences probably didn't buy a ticket to
"Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse" specifically

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.420
because they love multiverses so much; it's
the fan-favorite character and the phenomenal

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.920
animation that are the stars of this series.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:57.620
But because multiversal storytelling has more
of a place in popular culture than ever before,

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:00.935
selling audiences on the fundamental concept
was easier than ever.

00:04:01.032 --> 00:04:05.640
While history shows that people will go see
a "Spider-Man" movie regardless of the reviews,

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.591
it certainly helps if the buzz prior to release
is as strong as it was "Spider-Man: Across

00:04:09.591 --> 00:04:10.650
the Spider-Verse."

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:15.849
On the heels of opening weekend, its Rotten
Tomatoes score jumped straight to 95 percent,

00:04:15.849 --> 00:04:19.259
making it one of the most acclaimed major
studio films of the year.

00:04:19.259 --> 00:04:21.820
Its audience score went even higher at 96
percent.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:26.390
Though "Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse"
settled into a slightly higher 97 percent

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:31.000
Rotten Tomatoes rating, many critics called
the sequel even better than the original in

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.000
their write-ups.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.390
Fan sentiment has been equally effusive, and
the only thing bringing down these rave reviews

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.110
is the film's abrupt ending.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:42.550
It's two hours and 30 minutes, yet is effectively
the first half of a story that will resolve

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.729
itself in next year's "Spider-Man: Beyond
the Spider-Verse."

00:04:46.014 --> 00:04:49.680
The release of "Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse"
was delayed twice.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.870
Initially, a teaser advertised a premiere
date of April 2022.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:59.000
That got bumped back to October, then Sony
execs finally settled on June 2, 2023.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.150
Though the postponements were frustrating
to fans eager to see Miles Morales in action

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:06.330
again, this might be a case of good things
coming to those who wait.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.100
The move gave the creative team more time
to get "Across the Spider-Verse" just right,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:13.130
and it provided the movie with a choice spot
on the calendar.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:17.110
With school letting out and families looking
for something to do, "Across the Spider-Verse"

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:20.000
only had "The Little Mermaid" in its second
week to grapple with.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.300
"The Super Mario Bros. Movie" and "Guardians
of the Galaxy Vol. 3" were still in theaters

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:28.000
at the time of its release, but they weren't
the juggernauts they were weeks prior.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.160
Coming later in June are DC's "The Flash"
and Pixar's "Elemental," both of which drop

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:33.860
into cinemas on the 16th.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:38.319
The former has strong buzz but a potential
problem to overcome in its embattled star,

00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:39.319
Ezra Miller.

00:05:39.319 --> 00:05:43.300
Bad reviews out of Cannes poured cold water
on the prospects of "Elemental."

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:47.570
Neither is tracking anywhere near the "Spider-Verse"
sequel's opening weekend totals, all of which

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:51.680
should allow the Spidey sequel additional
weeks to dominate the box office and pad out

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:52.876
its total earnings.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:57.910
While most theatrical animated movies these
days are rated PG, the vast majority of superhero

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:00.240
and comic book movies are rated PG-13.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.590
That PG-13 rating, however, doesn't necessarily
stop parents from bringing younger children

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:09.180
to see, say, the latest installment of the
MCU.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:13.651
Sometimes word gets out that a PG-13 film
is too much for some kids to handle.

00:06:13.651 --> 00:06:17.520
"Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness"
and "Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3" both

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.620
pushed the envelope when it came to horror,
adult situations and humor.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:23.139
"Okay, now what?"

00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:24.870
"Open the f—ing door."

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:28.960
Like the original, "Spider-Man: Across the
Spider-Verse" is rated PG, but brilliantly

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.639
positions itself in the gray area between
an eye-dazzling kids' cartoon and more adult-oriented,

00:06:33.639 --> 00:06:35.810
dark superhero territory.

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:39.700
This gives parents of children who might still
be too young for some of Marvel's racier titles

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:40.700
peace of mind.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:45.000
Sure, the artistry and the emotionality are
far more mature than the average animated

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:49.650
movie, but there's minimal objectionable language,
no gross out moments, and no blood.

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:53.450
"Across the Spider-Verse" is packed full of
good role models and positive messages about

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:55.520
what it means to be a kid and a parent.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.370
Its well-deserved PG rating, plus its overall
quality and relevant themes, make it perfect

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:01.698
for a family outing.

00:07:01.854 --> 00:07:06.340
Just because "Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse"
is appropriate for single digit-aged audience

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:09.190
members doesn't mean older teens and adults
won't be entertained.

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:10.550
Yes, it's a cartoon.

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:13.720
Yes, it has messages about family and identity.

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:17.940
But absolutely nothing about "Across the Spider-Verse"
is simple or sugar-coated.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.699
Visually and narratively, the film is equal
parts authentic and edgy.

00:07:21.699 --> 00:07:26.925
Miles and Gwen face complex and sometimes
gut-wrenching problems that don't have obvious solutions.

00:07:26.925 --> 00:07:30.630
Whole stretches of the movie are downright
sad, which is skillfully reflected in the

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:32.810
animation style and color palette.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:36.240
The humor is also sharper than one would expect
from a PG movie.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.060
Writers Phil Lord and Christopher Miller are
known for being able to deliver rapid-fire

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:46.333
gags, as they did in "The LEGO Movie"— another
PG rated film that manages to be PG-13 style funny.

00:07:46.333 --> 00:07:51.120
The jokes are smart, subversive, and fun,
and the plot, which is remarkably unpredictable

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.460
for a kids movie, keeps fans on their toes
and trying to guess what's going to happen next.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:08:00.090
The film's PG rating welcomes a younger subset
of viewers, while doing nothing to deter the

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:05.000
demographic that usually prefers more complex
PG-13 and R-rated movies.

00:08:05.293 --> 00:08:10.437
Kid movies and superhero movies are perfect
opportunities for merchandising and product placement.

00:08:10.437 --> 00:08:15.000
Every time one comes out, brand new affiliated
items can be found on store shelves and at

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.060
fast food restaurants.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:18.670
"Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse" is no
different.

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:23.060
Tied to its release, Burger King offered a
limited time BK-Verse menu that featured a

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:26.130
red-bunned Whopper and a Spider-Verse ice
cream sundae.

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:28.060
The Whopper sold out within days.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:32.490
Selling out even faster were new "Across the
Spider-Verse"-branded Air Jordan 1 "Next Chapter"

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.460
high tops, gone in less than an hour.

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:36.060
"Are those my Jordans?"

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:38.380
"I can't help it if we're the same size."

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:42.779
Merch builds hype among the most devoted fans
and collectors, but not everyone cares about

00:08:42.779 --> 00:08:44.390
these kinds of promotions.

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:48.630
Just because Burger King, LEGO, and Nike are
household name brands doesn't mean everyone

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:52.570
is desperate for a red hamburger bun or a
pair of $200 sneakers.

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:55.839
These types of promotions do, however, get
a film's name out there.

00:08:55.839 --> 00:09:00.940
Logos and images are plastered on store windows
and internet browsers, and people subconsciously

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.364
become aware that a movie like "Across the
Spider-Verse" is coming soon.

00:09:04.481 --> 00:09:08.050
For all the planning that goes into making
a movie, there's one thing that matters that's

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:10.800
almost beyond the creators' control – is
it cool?

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:14.460
The cool factor is kind of intangible, and
it's not the kind of thing that you can force.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:19.220
If a piece of pop culture becomes cool to
engage with, talk about, make memes about,

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:23.519
its impact on pop culture gets much larger;
as it builds an audience, no one wants to

00:09:23.519 --> 00:09:24.519
be left out.

00:09:24.519 --> 00:09:27.210
"Across the Spider-Verse" is undeniably cool.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:30.290
But this impossibly high cool factor didn't
come out of nowhere.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:34.460
Miles' Jordans and Gwen's Converse are well-established,
eternally cool brands.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:36.870
The creative flourishes and in-jokes are cool.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:38.320
Lord and Miller are cool.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:41.050
The voice actors all range from well known
to beloved.

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:45.220
The original soundtrack by Metro Boomin and
a Spider-Verse-full of famous collaborators

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:46.690
is pretty cool, as well.

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:50.640
But perhaps the coolest thing about "Across
the Spider-Verse" is a new, surefire breakout

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:53.140
fan favorite character, Spider-Punk.

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:57.019
Otherwise known as Hobie Brown, Spider-Punk
is depicted in a faded out two dimensional

00:09:57.019 --> 00:10:01.690
collage of poster scraps, reminiscent of the
Sex Pistols aesthetic, among others.

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:05.740
Played by Oscar winner Daniel Kaluuya, he
has a thick cockney accent, and when he takes

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:08.660
his mask off, Miles can't believe he's even
cooler underneath.

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:12.680
It's no surprise that the terms "Hobie" and
"Spider-Punk" were trending on Twitter upon

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:16.974
the film's release, or that fan art is already
proliferating across the internet.

00:10:16.974 --> 00:10:21.700
Most people who saw "Across the Spider-Verse"
on opening weekend only saw it once, but you

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.800
can bet many will be seeing it again.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:27.200
This is one sequel that begs to be rewatched,
for multiple reasons.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:31.730
First, the art, the music, the script, and
the voice acting are so layered and complex,

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:35.090
fans want to pull further on some of the many
threads that appear only briefly.

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:39.060
Second, though there are moments where the
movie slows down to have a moment of human

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:43.140
connection, most of it moves so fast, audiences
leave a first viewing feeling as though they

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:44.400
must have missed something.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:48.709
The sequel is overflowing with Easter eggs,
callbacks, and cameos; sometimes multiple

00:10:48.709 --> 00:10:50.540
references share the same frame.

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:55.430
There are allusions to the first movie, allusions
to the live-action "Spider-Man" movies, allusions

00:10:55.430 --> 00:11:00.140
to Lord and Miller's other movies, nods to
comic books, nods to video games, as-of-yet

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:05.029
unseen variants of Spider-Man, and the number
42 appears more times than anyone could reasonably

00:11:05.029 --> 00:11:06.079
catch the first time around.

00:11:06.079 --> 00:11:10.370
There are even editor notes, like in the comics,
which sometimes appear and vanish too quickly

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:13.339
for viewers who don't yet have the benefit
of a pause button.

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:17.660
Taken as a whole, this ridiculous level of
"rewatchability" should help "Across the Spider-Verse"

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:21.262
stay at or near the top of the box office
throughout the first half of the summer.

